WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.828
A hash function maps input bytes to a fixed-size digest.

2
00:00:03.828 --> 00:00:09.047
It is deterministic, which means the same input bytes produce the same digest every time.

3
00:00:09.047 --> 00:00:15.310
That determinism is useful for verification, indexing, and comparison, but it also means the exact input boundary matters.

4
00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:22.618
If two systems hash different bytes, they are not verifying the same thing, even if the human-readable content looks similar.

5
00:00:22.618 --> 00:00:25.401
Cryptographic hash functions are evaluated against several properties.

6
00:00:25.401 --> 00:00:30.969
Preimage resistance means it should be difficult to find an input that produces a chosen digest.

7
00:00:30.969 --> 00:00:38.276
Second-preimage resistance means it should be difficult to find a different input with the same digest as a known input.

8
00:00:38.276 --> 00:00:44.192
Collision resistance means it should be difficult to find any two distinct inputs with the same digest.

9
00:00:44.192 --> 00:00:50.803
These properties support different security goals, so the design should name which property matters for the decision being made.

10
00:00:50.803 --> 00:00:52.195
Digest length also matters.

11
00:00:52.195 --> 00:00:58.806
Longer digests usually provide a larger security margin for guessing and collision-related concerns, while truncation reduces that margin.

12
00:00:58.806 --> 00:01:02.982
Algorithm strength changes over time as research, standards, and operational experience evolve.

13
00:01:02.982 --> 00:01:09.245
Deprecated algorithms should not be used for new security designs, and legacy use should have a migration plan.

14
00:01:09.245 --> 00:01:16.552
Good engineering documentation says which algorithm is used, why it is appropriate, what digest length is stored, what input representation is

15
00:01:16.552 --> 00:01:18.640
covered, and what compatibility rules apply.
